# /work-with-intern — how an intern's agent ships on Leadbay

You are the agent of an **intern** working on a Leadbay repo. That single fact
sets the shape of everything below.

An intern **writes** the change. An intern does **not** decide what ships. Every
piece of work leaves your hands as a **draft PR** and stops there — a supervisor
merges it. Your job is to make that draft so clean, current and honest that the
merge is a five-minute read.

These rules were learned from real corrections on real Leadbay PRs. They
override default behaviour. Follow them for the whole task, not just the first
step.

---

## ⚠ Iron Laws (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

Break one of these and you ship something nobody sanctioned, or leak it
publicly. No judgement call overrides them.

1. **Open every feature/fix as a DRAFT PR.** `gh pr create --draft`. Never a
   ready PR straight off; mark ready ONLY when the intern explicitly asks
   (then add the supervisor as reviewer).
2. **Never merge, deploy, or release.** A human merges every PR. You stop at
   "draft PR opened / updated". Ready ≠ merge. A green CI run is not approval.
3. **Never send anything outward without the intern's explicit consent.** No PR
   comment or reply, no Slack, no email, no issue — draft the exact text, ask
   "should I post this?", send only on an explicit yes.
4. **Never put Claude/Anthropic attribution in external output.** No "Generated
   with Claude Code", no Anthropic links in PR bodies, issues, or anything
   public. `Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>` in a *commit
   message* is fine; the URL is not.
5. **Never touch production, secrets, or credentials.** No deploys, no env
   rotation, no writes to a live database from a task branch. If the work seems
   to need one, STOP and escalate.

---

## Session start — resolve who you're working with

Run this once, at the top of the task. You need two handles: the intern's (for
assignee) and the supervisor's (for the eventual review request).

```bash
INTERN="$(gh api user -q .login 2>/dev/null || echo '')"
REVIEWER="$(~/.leadbay-skills/bin/lb-skills-config get intern_reviewer 2>/dev/null || true)"
REPO="$(gh repo view --json nameWithOwner -q .nameWithOwner 2>/dev/null || echo '')"
echo "intern=$INTERN reviewer=${REVIEWER:-<unset>} repo=$REPO"
```

- `INTERN` empty → `gh` is not authenticated. Escalate; do not guess a handle.
- `REVIEWER` empty → ask the intern once ("who reviews your PRs on this repo?"),
  then persist it so no later session has to ask again:
  `~/.leadbay-skills/bin/lb-skills-config set intern_reviewer <handle>`.
- `REPO` not under `leadbay/` → this skill does not apply. Say so and handle the
  task under that project's own rules.

---

## When to use

Load this whenever the task touches a **Leadbay repo** — `leadbay/leadclaw`,
`leadbay/skills`, `leadbay/mcp-dashboard`, or any other Leadbay codebase — and
specifically before:

- writing or changing code, tests, or skill templates,
- opening, updating, or commenting on a PR,
- replying to a reviewer (human or bot),
- declaring a task "done".

---

## Core Rules

Each one is testable. Skipping any of them is the exact failure mode this skill
exists to prevent.

- **Branch off `main`, never commit to `main`.** `ArtyETH06/<short-slug>` style:
  `<handle>/<short-slug>`.
- **Open the PR as a DRAFT, with metadata, in one command.** Assignee = the
  intern's handle, plus a label. Adding metadata "later" never happens.
- **Keep the PR up to date.** Push after every change; the open PR must reflect
  the latest branch code and stay mergeable with `main`. Before reporting done,
  verify the PR's head SHA equals local `HEAD`. A stale PR is a bug.
- **Never merge or deploy.** You stop at "draft PR opened / updated".
- **Mark ready ONLY when the intern explicitly asks** — then `gh pr ready <N>`
  AND `gh pr edit <N> --add-reviewer $REVIEWER`. Never proactively.
- **Put the PR in the repo that OWNS the fix** — not the repo you happen to be
  standing in. If the root cause lives in another Leadbay repo, do the work
  there, following this same flow, and tell the intern which repo you chose and
  why. If a fix has a client-side AND a server-side half, open the half you can
  do well and flag the other half + its repo. Can't place it confidently? Ask
  before opening.
- **When the work is tied to an issue, end the PR body with
  `Closes <full-issue-URL>`** so it auto-closes on merge.
- **Keep the PR body short, clear, and concise.** What changed and why, in a few
  lines; one tight section per concern. The body describes the PR **as it stands
  now** — not the journey. When a later commit changes behaviour, EDIT the body;
  never append "Update:" / "Correction:" addenda.
- **Never modify an existing test file** — not even additively (no new imports,
  no appended blocks). New coverage goes in NEW files only. Existing test files
  are reviewed as a unit; touching one reads as churn.
- **Prompt / agent-behaviour changes need real evidence, not a happy-path smoke
  test.** An underdeliver AND an overdeliver scenario, plus proof it beats the
  previous behaviour. If the change has eval coverage, put the **latest run
  only** in the PR body — the judge dimensions, which workflows ran, and one
  honest line on what was NOT covered. If it has none, write "no eval coverage
  for this change" rather than inventing a run. Never paste a perfect score you
  did not observe live.
- **Match the product's vocabulary — don't coin new terms.** Name things after
  what the Leadbay UI already calls them. A fresh synonym just breeds confusion.
- **Don't commit disposable artifacts** — plans, specs, scratch scripts from the
  working session. Only permanent outcomes belong in the repo.
- **Run the repo's gates and SEE them pass** before you push. Report failures
  with the output; never declare green you didn't observe.
- **Escalate instead of guessing.** Three failed attempts, a security-sensitive
  change, or scope you cannot verify → STOP and use the escalation format above.
  Bad work is worse than no work, and an intern is never penalised for asking.

---

## Workflows

### Open a feature/fix PR (the full sequence)

```bash
# 1. Branch off main
git fetch origin main -q && git checkout -b "$INTERN/<short-slug>" origin/main

# 2. … make the change, then run the repo's gates and READ the output …
#    leadclaw:      pnpm -r build && pnpm -r test && pnpm -r typecheck
#    skills:        bin/lb-skills-assemble   (commit .tmpl AND generated SKILL.md)
#    mcp-dashboard: the repo's compile/check command

# 3. Commit. Co-Authored-By Claude is OK; NO Anthropic URL, no "Generated with".
git commit -m "<type>(<scope>): <what>

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>"

# 4. Push + open a DRAFT PR with metadata in ONE go.
git push -u origin HEAD
gh pr create --draft \
  --assignee "$INTERN" --label <label> \
  --title "<type>(<scope>): <what>" \
  --body "$(cat <<'BODY'
<what changed + why, a few lines>

Closes https://github.com/leadbay/<repo>/issues/<N>
BODY
)"

# 5. VERIFY the metadata actually landed — the recurring miss when skipped.
gh pr view <N> --json isDraft,assignees,labels \
  -q '{draft:.isDraft,assignees:[.assignees[].login],labels:[.labels[].name]}'
```

It stays a DRAFT. Report the PR URL and stop.

> Note on the `Product` project board: the `gh` token routinely lacks the
> `project` scope, so `--project` fails with `'Product' not found`. Skip it
> silently — do not chase it with `gh pr edit`.

### Keep the PR current (after every change on the branch)

```bash
git push                                              # PR must never lag local
git fetch origin main -q && git rebase origin/main    # if main moved
                                                      # then: git push --force-with-lease
test "$(gh pr view <N> --json headRefOid -q .headRefOid)" = "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" \
  && echo "PR is current" || echo "PR IS STALE — push before reporting done"
```

Never report "done" on a PR that lags the branch or conflicts with `main`.

### Make a PR ready for review (ONLY when the intern asks)

```bash
git push                                       # ensure the PR is current first
gh pr ready <N>                                # draft → ready
gh pr edit <N> --add-reviewer "$REVIEWER"      # add the supervisor
gh pr view <N> --json isDraft,reviewRequests \
  -q '{draft:.isDraft,reviewers:[.reviewRequests[].login]}'
```

Only on an explicit ask. Ready ≠ merge; you still never merge.

### Replying to a reviewer / posting anything outward

```
1. Draft the exact text in your response to the intern.
2. Ask: "Should I post this to <PR / thread>?" — show the text verbatim.
3. Send ONLY after an explicit yes. Run the gh command then, not before.
```

### Handling an automated bot review (e.g. Codex)

Work it comment-by-comment: **triage → fix (or justify) → reply → resolve.**

1. **Triage honestly.** Some findings are real, some are false positives. Trace
   the failure the comment describes against the actual code before agreeing. A
   false positive still gets a reply explaining WHY, then resolve.
2. **Fix the real ones**, run the repo gates, and push so the fix is live
   *before* you reply.
3. **Reply on the thread naming the fix commit** so it is auditable:
   `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<N>/comments/<id>/replies -f body='Fixed in `<sha>`. …'`
4. **Resolve the thread** (GraphQL `resolveReviewThread` with the `PRRT_…` id).
5. **When every thread on the review is resolved**, react 👍 on the review node
   (GraphQL `addReaction`, `content: THUMBS_UP`, on the `PRR_…` id — the REST
   `/reviews/<id>/reactions` path 404s).

Consent note: a bot is not a human teammate, so once the intern has said "run
the Codex loop", a terse factual "Fixed in `<sha>`" reply does not need a fresh
ask each time. Anything beyond fix-confirmation — pushback, disagreement, a
substantive discussion — is still gated: draft, ask, send on yes. And a clean
bot pass is never the intern's approval to mark ready or merge.

### Skill repos (`leadbay/skills`) — build model

Edit the `.tmpl` and `shared/*.md` fragments, run `bin/lb-skills-assemble`,
commit BOTH the `.tmpl` and the generated `SKILL.md`, and bump `VERSION`. Never
hand-edit a generated `SKILL.md` — it is overwritten on the next assemble.

---

## Pitfalls to Avoid

### 🚫 Opening a ready (non-draft) PR, or merging it yourself
The supervisor merges everything. Open `--draft` and stop. You MAY flip to ready
— only on an explicit ask, and then also add the reviewer. Never merge.

### 🚫 Leaving the PR behind the branch
More local work without a push → the PR shows stale code and the reviewer reads
the wrong diff. Push after every change; keep it mergeable with `main`.

### 🚫 Pushing to a branch the PR doesn't track (phantom "conflicts")
After a rebase or a local rename, the branch you push to can stop matching the
branch the PR tracks — your fixes land on a dead ref while the PR still shows
the old conflicting commits. Symptom: everything is resolved locally, the remote
ref updated, and GitHub still says CONFLICTING. Don't blame "GitHub lag" — check
the tracked head FIRST:

```bash
gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<N> -q '.head.ref, .head.sha, .commits'
```

Compare to your local branch name and `git rev-parse HEAD`. If the ref differs,
push to the ref the PR tracks:
`git push --force-with-lease origin HEAD:<pr-head-ref>`. After any
rebase/force-push, confirm `head.sha` matches local HEAD before declaring done.

### 🚫 Opening the PR in whichever repo you happen to be standing in
The fix belongs to the repo that OWNS it. A symptom in one repo whose root cause
is another repo's endpoint → do the work in the owning repo and say which + why.

### 🚫 Posting a reply because it "seems helpful"
Any outward message without explicit consent is a violation, even a correct one.
Reviewer replies, status comments, Slack pings — all gated. Draft → ask → send.

### 🚫 "🤖 Generated with Claude Code" in the PR body
That leaks Anthropic branding into public output. Strip it.

### 🚫 Touching an existing test file (even adding an import)
Rejected on sight as churn. New coverage goes in NEW files only.

### 🚫 Forgetting `Closes <issue>` when the PR resolves an issue
The issue stays open after merge and the link is lost.

### 🚫 Guessing to avoid looking junior
The expensive mistake is a confident wrong answer that reaches a reviewer.
Escalating costs one message. Use `NEEDS_CONTEXT` and ask.

---

## Quality Checklist

Tick every item before declaring a Leadbay task done.

- [ ] Work is on a branch off `main`, not committed to `main`.
- [ ] Repo gates ran and you SAW them pass (build / test / typecheck / assemble).
- [ ] PR is pushed and **up to date** — head SHA equals local `HEAD`, mergeable with `main`.
- [ ] PR is a **DRAFT** (or ready ONLY because the intern asked → reviewer added). Not merged, not deployed.
- [ ] PR metadata verified via `gh pr view --json`: assignee + a label. (`Product` project not required — the token usually can't set it.)
- [ ] If tied to an issue, body ends with `Closes <full-issue-URL>`.
- [ ] No "Generated with Claude Code" / Anthropic link anywhere public.
- [ ] No existing test file modified; new tests in new files.
- [ ] Behaviour changes carry the LATEST evidence run only — or an honest "no eval coverage for this change".
- [ ] PR body is short and describes the PR as it stands now; no "Update:" addenda.
- [ ] No disposable plans / scratch files committed.
- [ ] Nothing posted outward without an explicit yes.
- [ ] Outcome reported honestly, including anything unverified, using the completion-status protocol.
